$BODY = <<<'HTML'
<h2>The conviction under appeal</h2>
<p>The appellant was convicted on 10 February 2023 by the Additional District and Sessions Judge / Special Judge (POCSO), Haridwar, in Special Sessions Trial No. 159 of 2021, for offences under Section 376AB of the Indian Penal Code and Sections 5(m) and 6 of the POCSO Act — provisions that carry among the heaviest minimum sentences in Indian criminal law. His jail appeal was filed in 2023 and has been admitted; the present application sought bail pending its final hearing. Mr. R.C. Tamta appeared as amicus curiae for the appellant.</p>

<h2>What troubled the Bench</h2>
<p>The FIR alleged that on 11 September 2021 the victim's clothes were found stained with blood and that the appellant had attempted to rape her. Against that stood the medical record.</p>

<div class="compare">
<div class="col old"><h3>The prosecution case</h3><p>Bloodstained clothes and the victim's account, accepted by the trial court as proof of an offence under Sections 5(m)/6 POCSO and 376AB IPC.</p></div>
<div class="arrow">→</div>
<div class="col new"><h3>The record on appeal</h3><p>The medical examination found no injury and the hymen intact; the State conceded there was no medical evidence of injury; the forensic report did not support the victim's account to the extent alleged.</p></div>
</div>

<p>The Division Bench of Justice Ravindra Maithani and Justice Siddhartha Sah recorded (para 9) that the matter was at the post-conviction bail stage and that its observations would have no bearing on the appeal. It acknowledged (para 10) that a conviction can rest on a victim's testimony alone where it inspires confidence — but found that the state of the medical and forensic evidence weighed in favour of suspension. Holding this a fit case (para 11), the Court suspended the sentence during the pendency of the appeal and directed release on a personal bond with two sureties of like amount (paras 12-14), with the appeal to be listed for final hearing.</p>

<h2>The larger point for POCSO appeals</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>Admission</strong> — The appeal is admitted; the court recognises arguable grounds exist.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Suspension application</strong> — A separate application under the appellate bail jurisdiction targets custody, not the conviction.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Tangible infirmity</strong> — The applicant points to concrete features: here, the gap between the allegation and the medical and forensic record.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Interim liberty</strong> — Sentence suspended, bail granted, observations insulated from the final hearing.</div>
</div>

<div class="check">
<p><strong>For appellants and their families:</strong> in POCSO convictions, the suspension application should be built on the documentary record — the MLC, the FSL report, the contradictions between them and the ocular account — rather than on sympathy or delay alone. Where the State's own record does not support the charge, saying so precisely, page and paragraph, is what moves a Division Bench.</p>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p>Nothing in the order dilutes the seriousness of POCSO offences or the weight of a child's testimony, which remains sufficient for conviction when it inspires confidence. The order operates at the intersection of two principles — the gravity of the offence and the right, under Article 21, not to serve a sentence that an admitted appeal may yet set aside — and resolves the tension on the specific state of this record.</p>
</div>
